\mt1 Psalms

\c 14

\d For the director of music. Of David.\f + \fr 14:0 \fk KONTEXT \fq For the director of music \ft The Hebrew superscription is *lamnatstsēaḥ ledāwid* — "for the lead player / musical director; for David." The musical instruction signals liturgical performance. The psalm's meter is qinah (3:2) — a funeral dirge rhythm. The object of the mourning is unusual: this is not a lament for the dead or for the speaker's own suffering, but a mock-elegy for the spiritual death of the exploiter. The compressed, staccato lines mourn what the powerful have become. — Psalm 53 is the same poem preserved independently in the Elohistic Psalter (Book II), with YHWH replaced by Elohim throughout. The differences between the two versions in vv. 5–6 suggest separate textual traditions.\f*

\s1 The Fool and the Poor

\b
\q1
\v 1 The fool says in his heart:\f + \fr 14:1 \fk KULTUR \fq The fool \ft The Hebrew word is *nābāl* — and it does not mean what modern English "fool" suggests. This is not an intellectual category. It is a moral-economic one. The *nābāl* is embodied in the Nabal of 1 Samuel 25: the wealthy, contemptuous social predator who refuses covenant obligation to the vulnerable — who denies that divine governance applies to how he treats those beneath him. The word's root (*nbl*) means "to wither, to fade" — moral collapse. Kidner: "an aggressive perversity, epitomized in the Nabal of 1 Samuel 25:25." — The Targum renders the fool's inner speech: "There is no rule of God in the land." Not metaphysical denial but political: divine governance over economic behavior is dismissed as irrelevant. The psalm's diagnosis is not about intellectual atheism — it is about the economic ideology that results when powerful people decide there will be no reckoning for how they treat the powerless. Every culture has this social type. The psalm names it.\f*
\q2 \it There is no God.\it*
\q1 They are corrupt. Their deeds are vile.
\q1 No one does good.

\b
\q1
\v 2 The LORD looks down from heaven\f + \fr 14:2 \fk KONTEXT \fq The LORD looks down from heaven \ft The divine gaze descending in three visual beats — heaven, then the children of earth, then looking for anyone with understanding — is a deliberate three-colon enjambment (Alter calls it "remarkable"). The reader follows God's eyes downward in a miniature narrative sequence. — The motif of divine surveillance appears throughout the ancient Near East: the deity enthroned in the heavens watching human conduct. But in standard royal ideology, divine surveillance legitimates the existing power structure. In Psalm 14, the divine gaze does the opposite: it exposes the corruption of the existing order. God looks down and finds everyone has gone wrong. The structural parallel is Genesis 11:5 (Babel) and 18:21 (Sodom) — divine descent to investigate, not to endorse.\f*
\q1 on the children of earth —
\q1 to see if there is one who understands,
\q2 one who seeks God.

\b
\q1
\v 3 All have turned away,
\q1 all gone rotten together.
\q1 No one does good.
\q1 Not even one.\f + \fr 14:3 \fk TEXT \fq Not even one \ft The phrase "no one does good" appeared already in v. 1. Its return in v. 3 is not mere repetition but what Hebrew poetry scholars call Kugelian intensification: the same statement returns after the divine investigation and is now confirmed and escalated — "not even one." The first occurrence was the psalmist's dismayed observation. The second is the divine verdict. Alter: "less a refrain than a dumfounded pronouncement." — Paul quotes vv. 1–3 in Romans 3:10–12, universalizing the statement into a claim about all humanity. Some Greek manuscripts of the Psalms (LXX) insert after v. 3 a lengthy expansion drawn from Psalms 5:9, 140:3, 10:7, and Isaiah 59:7–8 — the catena Paul uses in Romans 3:13–18. Scholarly consensus: this expansion was added by Christian scribes retrogressively from Romans 3. It is not part of the original Hebrew psalm. Origen recognized this in his Hexapla. Aperto follows the Hebrew text without the expansion.\f*

\s1 Devourers

\b
\q1
\v 4 Do they not know — all who deal in harm?\f + \fr 14:4 \fk KULTUR \fq who devour my people — devour them like bread \ft The Hebrew verb *ʾākal* (to eat, to devour) appears twice in this verse — deliberately. "Those who devour my people have eaten bread." The same verb is applied to the consumption of human lives and the consumption of lunch. The juxtaposition is the rhetorical point: these are people who destroy others with the same casualness with which they eat their dinner. Goldingay: "Like a contract killer going out to dinner." — The prophetic parallel is Micah 3:1–3: rulers who "eat the flesh of my people, flay their skin from them, break their bones, and chop them up like meat in a kettle." The image was recognizable across the ancient world as a description of elite economic predation — what Hossfeld/Zenger (citing H. W. Wolff) call "Wohlstandskannibalismus": the cannibalism of the comfortable. The powerful extract from the vulnerable as naturally as they reach for bread. No thought. No pause. Just appetite.\f*
\q1 They devour my people —
\q1 devour them like bread.
\q1 They have not called on the LORD.

\s1 Terror

\b
\q1
\v 5 There — terror gripped them,
\q1 terror.\f + \fr 14:5 \fk TEXT \fq terror gripped them, terror \ft The Hebrew is *pāḥădû pāḥad* — verb and cognate noun together: "they feared a fear" or "terror seized them with terror." This is an emphatic intensification construction — the Hebrew equivalent of doubling for emphasis. The translation preserves the doubling effect with the standalone second "terror." — The verb is in prophetic perfect tense: the judgment is announced as if already accomplished. This is not a wish for what will happen or a description of what is happening now. It is the prophetic announcement of divine certainty — the kind of speech that speaks the future as a settled reality. The exploiters' confidence will collapse. The prophetic voice declares it done.\f*
\q1 For God is with the righteous.

\b
\q1
\v 6 You would shame the wisdom of the lowly —\f + \fr 14:6 \fk TEXT \fq the wisdom of the lowly \ft Two translation decisions converge here. First: *ʿēṣāh* is wisdom-register vocabulary — it means counsel, strategy, discernment. "Plans of the poor" (as many translations render it) vastly undersells the term. The poor are not merely making plans; they possess *wisdom* — the kind of strategic discernment the powerful try to crush. The poor are agents with their own knowledge, not passive objects of pity. — Second: the verse's grammar is a crux. The Masoretic text reads *tābîšû* ("you put to shame"), which makes the verse awkward. Hossfeld and Zenger prefer the emendation *tōḇîšû* ("you will come to grief"), yielding an ironic reversal: "You will come to grief over the wisdom of the poor, for the LORD is their refuge." The oppressors' attempt to frustrate the poor's discernment rebounds on them. Aperto follows this emendation because it captures the structural theology of the psalm: divine governance protects the wisdom the vulnerable carry. The powerful cannot ultimately defeat it.\f*
\q1 but the LORD is their refuge.

\s1 Longing

\b
\q1
\v 7 O that Israel's rescue would come from Zion!\f + \fr 14:7 \fk KULTUR \fq Jacob will rejoice. Israel will be glad. \ft The psalm ends not in triumph but in longing — the Hebrew optative *mî yittēn* ("who will give" = "O that it would happen") is an unfulfilled wish. The corruption of vv. 1–3 has not ceased. The devouring of v. 4 continues. The divine judgment of v. 5 is spoken in prophetic certainty but has not yet arrived visibly. The psalm's ending is emotionally honest: desire, not achievement. — "Jacob" and "Israel" are both names for the covenant people — the specifically Jewish community whose liberation the psalm hopes for. This is Israel's own national restoration prayer, composed within and for Israel. The psalm must not be read supersessionistically as referring to "the church" or "God's people" in a way that erases the Jewish national referents. Augustine's reading of v. 7 as the final gathering of Israel into the Gentile church is a historically documented supersessionist interpretation that Aperto does not follow.\f*
\q1 When the LORD restores his people —
\q1 Jacob will rejoice. Israel will be glad.
